program            = 003_wide_and_unicode_literals
area               = 11_literals_and_strings
description        = Wide, UTF-8, 16-bit and 32-bit string and character literals, indexed in place with every element cast explicitly
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- No string literal is ever modified. Every literal is read only, through indexing.
- No signed integer overflow. Every code point is at most 0x7FFF and every element is
  widened to long long or unsigned long long, both 64-bit on all four targets, before
  being printed. Loop counters are int and bounded by a literal element count.
- No shift operations of any kind, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No out-of-bounds access. Each loop bound is the literal's own element count,
  computed as sizeof literal divided by sizeof of its first element, so the loop
  visits exactly the elements including the terminating null and never one past it.
  The explicit terminator reads index count-1, which is the last valid element.
- No aliasing violation. The UTF-8 literal's elements are read and converted through
  unsigned char, which is always permitted; the other literals are read through their
  own element type by indexing the literal directly.
- No uninitialized read. Every value read is an element of a string literal, and zero
  has a static initializer.
- Every universal-character-name obeys C11 6.4.3p2: no UCN specifies a short
  identifier below 00A0 other than 0024. Plain ASCII characters are written directly
  rather than as UCNs, because a UCN for them would be ill-formed.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points; no argument to any call has a
  side effect; nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of unrelated
  objects; no address or pointer value is printed.
- Verified empirically: the default strict warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic
  -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror) produces zero diagnostics, and
  -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all runs to completion with no
  output and exit status 0. -pedantic is retained deliberately: the four literal
  prefixes are standard C11, not GNU extensions.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
The three C11 character type names are never written anywhere in the program, not
even in a comment, and the hazards they represent are handled by construction rather
than by a divergence marker:

1. The wide character type's signedness and width are implementation-defined, and
   this repository fixes them for no supported target: recursive, case-insensitive
   searches of docs/ for wchar, char16, char32 and unicode return zero matches. No
   per-target table is given here, and that omission is deliberate. One measured on
   this machine would describe the reference toolchain installed today rather than
   any contract bcc documents, and citing it would make this program depend on a
   property no repository artifact settles. The dependence is removed instead: every
   wide element is indexed in place and cast explicitly to long long before
   printing, which is correct whichever underlying type the implementation chose.
   tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md section 5.3 records the same decision
   and the same reason.
2. The 16-bit and 32-bit character type names cannot be named in THIS program at
   all: they are uchar.h typedefs, and neither uchar.h nor wchar.h is among bcc's
   nine required bundled freestanding headers (stddef.h, stdint.h, stdarg.h,
   stdbool.h, limits.h, float.h, stdalign.h, stdnoreturn.h, iso646.h). Two header
   exception classes are sanctioned in the corpus and only two, and neither reaches
   those headers: the programs in area 07_variadics may include stdarg.h and nothing
   else, and the dedicated bundled-header probe
   12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c may include exactly those nine,
   with the bonus stdatomic.h excluded from both. This program takes neither
   exception and includes no header at all, which is the corpus default, so the two
   type names are simply unavailable to it. Every 16-bit and 32-bit element is
   therefore indexed in place and cast explicitly to unsigned long long.
3. All code point values are restricted to the range 0x00 to 0x7FFF, so no printed
   value can be affected by the signedness difference in point 1: every value is
   positive in every candidate element type.
4. The UTF-8 literal's element type is plain char, whose signedness is itself
   implementation-defined (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and
   riscv64), so each UTF-8 code unit is converted through unsigned char before being
   widened.
5. Array extents are printed as element counts, computed as sizeof literal divided by
   sizeof of its first element, never as byte counts, so no element-width assumption
   reaches the output. long long and unsigned long long are 64-bit on all four
   targets, and no long, size_t or pointer is printed, so the i686 width difference
   (sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 elsewhere) has no effect.
6. THE MAPPING OF A UNIVERSAL-CHARACTER-NAME TO THE EXTENDED EXECUTION CHARACTER SET is
   implementation-defined under C11, and it is what every wide (L) value printed by this
   program is. This is the one implementation-defined property the output genuinely
   depends on, and it is the reason a conforming backend could otherwise be mislabelled:
   an implementation that mapped U+00A9 to anything but 0xA9 would still be conforming,
   yet a cross-backend comparison against a target that maps it to 0xA9 would report a
   divergence that is not a defect. The program therefore does not assume the mapping --
   it PINS it with _Static_assert on every wide value it uses (0x41, 0x24, 0xA9, 0x100,
   0x7FF, 0x7FFF and 0x7A), so an implementation whose mapping differs is refused at
   compile time with a message naming the expected value, instead of silently producing a
   number the oracles would then have to attribute. Verified as a negative control:
   changing one asserted value makes the translation fail with "static assertion failed:
   wide execution character set: U+00A9 must map to 0xA9". The 16-bit and 32-bit values
   are pinned the same way; those are SPECIFIED by C11 6.4.5 as UTF-16 and UTF-32 rather
   than implementation-defined, and are asserted only so that an incorrect encoding is
   also refused at compile time rather than after the fact.

MEASURED WIDE EXECUTION CHARACTER SET, per target, with the suite's toolchain of record,
which is what makes cross-backend comparison of the wide values sound:

  target    __STDC_ISO_10646__   sizeof(wchar_t)   wchar_t signedness   U+00A9 -> value
  x86_64    201706               4                 signed               169
  i686      201706               4                 signed               169
  aarch64   201706               4                 unsigned             169
  riscv64   201706               4                 signed               169

All four define __STDC_ISO_10646__, which is the standard's own way for an implementation
to document that wchar_t values are Unicode code points, and all four map every universal
character name in this program to its code point. Because the mapping agrees on all four
and is pinned by the asserts, oracle (b) remains ENABLED for this program rather than
restricted. The wchar_t signedness difference on aarch64 is real but cannot reach the
output, for the reason given in point 3.

__STDC_ISO_10646__ IS DELIBERATELY NOT USED AS A COMPILE-TIME GATE. Requiring it was
tried and rejected on measurement: the alternate reference compiler maps every
universal-character-name in this program to its correct code point while defining no such
macro at all, so gating on
the advertisement rather than on the behaviour would refuse a compiler that is doing
exactly the right thing -- and would refuse the compiler under test for a property the
suite never needed it to announce. The macro is recorded above as corroborating evidence;
the asserts are the gate, because they test the property the printed values actually
depend on.

Documentation note, recorded for reference only. Support for the u8, u, U and L
literal prefixes is not enumerated in the documented literal inventory.
docs/technical-specifications.md line 99 and line 498 list numeric and string or
character literal parsing with escape sequences and the u, l, ll and f suffixes, but
none of the four prefixes; line 497's 44-keyword C11 list contains neither the
16-bit nor the 32-bit character type name; and recursive searches of docs/ for
unicode, char16, char32 and wchar return no matches.

That is an INVENTORY OMISSION, and an inventory omission is not a documented
limitation. No marker is attached, and none may be minted on this basis alone, for
two independent reasons. First, this program does not diverge, and a marker on a
program that does not diverge yields an unexpected success, which fails the run.
Second, and the binding reason, a marker's basis must be an artifact that EXPLICITLY
documents the limitation: silence about a feature is not a statement about it, so an
omission can never serve as a basis however many inventories omit it.

Two conditions must therefore BOTH hold before a marker could exist here. A
repository artifact must explicitly document the limitation, AND a real divergence
must have been observed and reproduced. Until both hold, any divergence in this
program is classified as a FINDING: kept as a verbatim reproducer with the captured
outputs from every compiler and backend, an environment fingerprint and the exact
reproduction commands, and reported rather than patched. If a marker ever does become
valid it must be minted in both places at once, here and in
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md, whose consistency is checked in both
directions. That register's section 4.3 records this same analysis and reaches the
same conclusion.

The source file is pure US-ASCII: every non-ASCII character appears only as a
universal-character-name escape.
END
